Control borrowed writes under backpressure, flush, close and early reply

A 64 KiB borrowed write meets real EAGAIN and still sends exact bytes; a
partly sent borrowed write is sent whole before its flush; a closed peer
ends the client with a stable error and no new borrow; and an early reply
to a write still being sent fails the client. The header now names a
successful flush and a terminal wire failure as release points, and
PROVENANCE records the slot and client layout growth. Review notes from
Codex.
